WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.110 --> 00:00:08.160
The frigid ocean of the video game Subnautica:
Below Zero teems with incredible life — from

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:11.940
gigantic herbivores to vicious carnivores.

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:18.090
Like the tropical seas of the game’s predecessor,
this environment feels truly unique: an undersea

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:24.840
world of bewildering landscapes, detailed
ecosystems, and surprising mysteries.

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:30.000
Many of the aliens in Below Zero were created
by Alex Ries, one of the most talented creature

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.310
designers out there.

00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:38.070
And unlike the first game, in Below Zero,
dangers lurk both above and below the water…

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:44.620
So, for this entry into the archive, we’ll
embark on one of our documentary-style expeditions,

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:47.850
charting biomes under the waves and upon ice;
encountering both friendly — and deadly

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:48.850
— forms of arctic life.

00:00:48.850 --> 00:00:53.961
This two-part series will analyze the biology
behind these puzzling aliens, and investigate

00:00:53.961 --> 00:00:56.579
how they compare to life in Earth’s oceans.

00:00:56.579 --> 00:01:01.380
And although this experience once again ranks
among the scariest I’ve had while doing

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:06.310
fieldwork for the Archive, I’m determined
to document this alien world firsthand.

00:01:06.310 --> 00:01:13.700
So, let’s dive in together, and explore
the freezing depths of Sector Zero.

00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:20.080
Sector Zero is a region of extremely low temperatures,
where organisms of all types have had to adapt

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:21.310
to the cold.

00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:26.450
Beginning on the ice floats above the shallows,
a curious lifeform makes its home in this

00:01:26.450 --> 00:01:28.940
region where land meets the sea.

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:32.760
Huddled together on a float is a flock of
waddling Pengwings.

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:38.110
Pengwings are small, predatory fauna which…
aren’t the most graceful on land.

00:01:38.110 --> 00:01:43.710
When a Pengwing dives underwater, however,
the creature’s unique retractable tail allows

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:47.250
them to twist through the currents with remarkable
grace.

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:53.490
Despite their appearance, Pengwings are carnivores,
and their beak opens horizontally, revealing

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:56.060
a rather alarming set of teeth.

00:01:56.060 --> 00:02:01.030
In their body shape and behavior, Pengwings
show a high degree of convergence with Earth

00:02:01.030 --> 00:02:06.490
penguins — with both species adapting to
an arctic environment in similar ways.

00:02:06.490 --> 00:02:12.720
And on any ice-float with Pengwings, you will
also find Penglings — the juvenile form

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:15.980
of Pengwings who never stray far from their
parents.

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:20.720
Even more than adults, infant Penglings are
extremely awkward on land.

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:26.430
But what they might lack in overall balance,
they make up for in endless enthusiasm.

00:02:26.430 --> 00:02:31.440
And while Penglings might possess the same,
somewhat alarming fanged mouth, they’re

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:37.930
remarkably docile creatures, perfectly content
with being gently lifted up.

00:02:37.930 --> 00:02:43.540
As nice as it is to briefly hold a baby Pengling,
however, I don’t want to disrupt their natural

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:45.450
routines…

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:51.230
As we dive into the polar waters, a shallow,
surprisingly warm region awaits just under

00:02:51.230 --> 00:02:52.530
the ice floats.

00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:59.010
The landscape of this coastal sea is dominated
by Coral Bridges, great arches formed from

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:05.340
rapidly growing coral polyps that can exceed
600 feet, or 200 meters in length.

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:09.569
Bioluminescent blue barnacles grow on the
Coral’s underside, which a scan suggests

00:03:09.569 --> 00:03:13.810
are actually a separate species taking advantage
of the bridges’ structure.

00:03:13.810 --> 00:03:18.860
But why would the coral bridges grow into
such strange, arching shapes?

00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:24.380
The answer lies in a phenomenon known as Thigmotropism
— a pattern of growth observed in Earth

00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:27.380
plants in response to touch.

00:03:27.380 --> 00:03:32.640
Thigmotropism is what allows vines to wrap
around and ‘climb’ up poles, and it’s

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:38.420
the likely reason the great Coral bridges
always grow from one rock to another…

00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:43.819
Just beyond these coral bridges, we encounter
an Arctic Peeper, who watches for predators

00:03:43.819 --> 00:03:46.860
with its massive yellow eyes.

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:51.489
Relatives of the Common Peeper in the tropical
seas, Arctic Peepers’ scales have evolved

00:03:51.489 --> 00:03:54.680
a whitish hue for better camouflage against
the ice.

00:03:54.680 --> 00:04:00.120
Other familiar herbivores also swim here,
like the translucent Bladderfish and the vibrant

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:01.140
hoopfish.

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:06.709
Together, these species form an important
base in the Sector Zero food chain for the

00:04:06.709 --> 00:04:12.540
larger, less familiar creatures that lurk
under the ice.

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:17.070
Swimming northwards, I’ve reached a region
known as the Sparse Arctic.

00:04:17.070 --> 00:04:23.210
This entire biome is situated under thick
ice shelves, and receives little direct sunlight.

00:04:23.210 --> 00:04:28.830
On the underside of these icebergs, hazardous
ice formations known as Brinicles protrude

00:04:28.830 --> 00:04:30.710
like jagged fangs.

00:04:30.710 --> 00:04:35.969
Highly unstable, Brinicles frequently shatter
and crash to the sea floor.

00:04:35.969 --> 00:04:42.699
Yet as foreboding as the Sparse Arctic seems,
some truly remarkable species thrive here…

00:04:42.699 --> 00:04:46.199
This odd-looking fellow is called a Brinewing.

00:04:46.199 --> 00:04:51.800
With its rotund form and beak-like mouth,
it gives the impression of an ineffectual

00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:52.800
herbivore.

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:55.069
But the Brinewing is a powerful predator.

00:04:55.069 --> 00:05:00.939
To hunt, it unleashes a rapid-fire torrent
of a supercooled saline solution that freezes

00:05:00.939 --> 00:05:04.430
in the water, and can encase its prey in ice.

00:05:04.430 --> 00:05:10.550
A scan suggests the Brinewing uses a highly-specialized
bladder to produce this supercooled brine

00:05:10.550 --> 00:05:12.889
from the surrounding salt water.

00:05:12.889 --> 00:05:18.500
As unusal an adaptation as this might seem,
the Earth Archerfish can shoot down insects

00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:24.900
using water jets launched from their specialized
mouths… and are remarkably accurate.

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:29.860
Other species in the Sparse Arctic have very
different survival strategies.

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:35.499
Like the enormous Titan Holefish — a slow
moving herbivore with a strange hole in the

00:05:35.499 --> 00:05:36.909
center of its body.

00:05:36.909 --> 00:05:42.819
The Titan Holefish is a relative of the tiny,
guppy-sized tropical holefish — and is so

00:05:42.819 --> 00:05:47.740
much larger than its cousin you can actually
swim through the hole at its center.

00:05:47.740 --> 00:05:50.110
But what is the purpose of this hole?

00:05:50.110 --> 00:05:56.319
A scan suggests the gel-like interior is lined
with hundreds of eggs, but curiously, their

00:05:56.319 --> 00:05:59.889
DNA doesn’t match that of the Holefish…

00:05:59.889 --> 00:06:05.639
The mystery of the eggs is revealed when I
spot a tiny lifeform swimming alongside the

00:06:05.639 --> 00:06:06.639
Holefish.

00:06:06.639 --> 00:06:12.009
Soon, many others join in, swarming like a
bee colony around a hive.

00:06:12.009 --> 00:06:16.830
These are Symbiotes — small predators that
have evolved to make their nests inside the

00:06:16.830 --> 00:06:23.270
hole of the Titan Holefish, and in turn defend
them at all costs — and even provide them

00:06:23.270 --> 00:06:24.339
with food.

00:06:24.339 --> 00:06:29.919
This highly cooperative lifestyle is an example
of a mutualistic partnership — one of the

00:06:29.919 --> 00:06:38.129
few on Planet 4546 B. As a result, the Titan
Holefish has evolved to be remarkably unintelligent:

00:06:38.129 --> 00:06:43.599
swimming forward on impulse, fully trusting
the complex ecosystem that supports its every

00:06:43.599 --> 00:06:46.369
need.

00:06:46.369 --> 00:06:52.300
Just beyond the Sparse Arctic, the bare sand
gives way to the teeming Arctic Kelp Forest.

00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:59.639
Here, Creepvine Plants sway in the polar current,
casting light into the depths with their bioluminescent

00:06:59.639 --> 00:07:00.639
seeds.

00:07:00.639 --> 00:07:05.319
These Creepvine plants are close relatives
of the green variety which grow in the warm

00:07:05.319 --> 00:07:10.309
waters of the tropics, although the chemical
composition of the polar waters has turned

00:07:10.309 --> 00:07:12.159
them a pale yellow.

00:07:12.159 --> 00:07:16.909
Among the fronds, familiar fish like the boomerang
flit between the leaves.

00:07:16.909 --> 00:07:21.039
…And some very unfamiliar fish swim here
also.

00:07:21.039 --> 00:07:26.990
These extremely bizarre lifeforms are called
Spinner Fish, and have perhaps the most peculiar

00:07:26.990 --> 00:07:30.240
method of swimming ever conceived.

00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:36.069
Using three pairs of siphons on the side of
their body, Spinner Fish jet along like living

00:07:36.069 --> 00:07:37.069
waterwheels.

00:07:37.069 --> 00:07:43.409
At a glance, its bizarre body the plan somewhat
resembles the larval form of the ocean sunfish.

00:07:43.409 --> 00:07:47.429
But ultimately, the Spinner Fish is one of
a kind.

00:07:47.429 --> 00:07:51.520
Speaking of one of a kind, I soon spot an
incredible lifeform.

00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:57.550
I’m just about to scan it when it swims
in and snatches the scanner from my hands.

00:07:57.550 --> 00:08:03.400
Frustrated, I give chase, and find the creature
looks back and waves the stolen scanner to

00:08:03.400 --> 00:08:04.400
mock me.

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:08.879
Thankfully I’m able to cut it off and snatch
my tool back.

00:08:08.879 --> 00:08:14.150
Finishing the scan, I discover this creature
might be annoying, but it’s truly remarkable,

00:08:14.150 --> 00:08:18.879
with both a developed brain and finger like
appendages for object manipulation.

00:08:18.879 --> 00:08:23.939
It’s called a Sea Monkey — a nickname
it shares with Earth brine shrimp.

00:08:23.939 --> 00:08:30.349
Unlike those Sea Monkeys, however, the Sea
Monkey of Planet 4546 B seems about as intelligent

00:08:30.349 --> 00:08:35.729
as an actual primate — meaning it’s the
smartest lifeform I’ve found in sector zero.

00:08:35.729 --> 00:08:40.579
But given time, the Sea Monkeys might have
an incredible future trajectory.

00:08:40.579 --> 00:08:44.610
I just hope they evolve to be less annoying…

00:08:44.610 --> 00:08:49.480
With the Kelp Forest thoroughly charted, it’s
time to regroup on the surface.

00:08:49.480 --> 00:08:54.420
Sticking my head above the water, however,
I find the shallows are engulfed in a dangerous

00:08:54.420 --> 00:08:56.029
polar storm.

00:08:56.029 --> 00:09:01.860
Looking around, I realize that the Pengwing
flock from earlier might be in serious danger.

00:09:01.860 --> 00:09:07.010
Upon arriving at their ice float, however,
I find the Pengwings unbothered by the weather,

00:09:07.010 --> 00:09:10.269
simply huddling for warmth and waiting out
the rain.

00:09:10.269 --> 00:09:13.160
Truly, Pengwings are intriguing creatures.

00:09:13.160 --> 00:09:17.190
With the sun thankfully returning, it occurs
to me that there’s much I still have to

00:09:17.190 --> 00:09:23.399
learn about Pengwing flock dynamics, but I
don’t want to disrupt them with my presence.

00:09:23.399 --> 00:09:24.880
Time for some innovation.

00:09:24.880 --> 00:09:30.240
I’ve devised an alternative, less conspicuous
method of observation.

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:35.810
This machine is my Spy Pengling, and it will
hopefully be able to keep an eye on the Pengwing

00:09:35.810 --> 00:09:40.889
flock with its hidden cameras while I continue
my expedition across Sector Zero.

00:09:40.889 --> 00:09:46.230
I’m uncertain if the Pengwings will be fooled
into accepting this robot as one of their

00:09:46.230 --> 00:09:49.670
own… but the only way to find out is to
test it.

00:09:49.670 --> 00:09:53.800
As inconspicuously as I can, I deploy the
robot.

00:09:53.800 --> 00:09:59.949
Taking control via an axillary remote, I navigate
the Spy Pengling into the midst of the flock.

00:09:59.949 --> 00:10:02.699
It’s… not a perfect disguise.

00:10:02.699 --> 00:10:06.189
But the Pengwings don’t seem particularly
bothered by its presence.

00:10:06.189 --> 00:10:11.139
Though it’s unlikely this robot is fooling
them, it doesn’t appear to disrupt their

00:10:11.139 --> 00:10:12.269
natural behavior.

00:10:12.269 --> 00:10:18.990
I’ll leave the flock under its watchful
eye, and head deeper into the unknown regions...

00:10:18.990 --> 00:10:25.680
Even further out to sea, the mysterious Lilypad
Islands hang in the water, seemingly by miraculous

00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:26.680
means.

00:10:26.680 --> 00:10:31.850
In truth, these floating chunks of rock are
held up by Giant Lilypads: an enormous type

00:10:31.850 --> 00:10:34.699
of plant that define this ecosystem.

00:10:34.699 --> 00:10:40.319
Giant Lilypads are indeed so large that it’s
possible to stand on their leafy tops without

00:10:40.319 --> 00:10:41.680
fear of sinking.

00:10:41.680 --> 00:10:46.269
But it’s the bizarre life that swims in
the lilypads’ shadows that make this region

00:10:46.269 --> 00:10:47.829
truly unique…

00:10:47.829 --> 00:10:52.640
Take, for example, the hilariously named Noot
Fish.

00:10:52.640 --> 00:10:58.740
With its colorful markings and innocent, beak-like
snout, the Noot fish can easily pass as a

00:10:58.740 --> 00:10:59.740
herbivore.

00:10:59.740 --> 00:11:03.540
But any fish that makes that mistake is soon
swallowed whole.

00:11:03.540 --> 00:11:09.319
The Noot Fish’s beak is in fact a stretchy,
extendable lip, lined with barb-like teeth

00:11:09.319 --> 00:11:12.200
it uses to envelop prey.

00:11:12.200 --> 00:11:17.620
This kind of extendible mouth somewhat resembles
that of the Sarcastic Fringehead of the Pacific

00:11:17.620 --> 00:11:18.670
Ocean.

00:11:18.670 --> 00:11:20.720
Best to keep an eye out.

00:11:20.720 --> 00:11:24.029
But there are bigger predators here than the
Noot Fish.

00:11:24.029 --> 00:11:29.280
The Brute Shark is an aggressive predator
that scours the depths with its ghostly white

00:11:29.280 --> 00:11:30.540
eyes.

00:11:30.540 --> 00:11:37.200
With huge fangs and a massive, bony jaw, they
feed on just about anything they catch.

00:11:37.200 --> 00:11:42.810
Like many Earth sharks, they prefer to attack
via ambush from above or below.

00:11:42.810 --> 00:11:47.630
The only good news is that while they’re
deadly, Brute Sharks aren’t particularly

00:11:47.630 --> 00:11:48.630
fast.

00:11:48.630 --> 00:11:52.910
Still, if I don’t keep my distance, I might
be on the menu...

00:11:52.910 --> 00:11:56.880
Closer to the surface, I spot a remarkably
colorful lifeform.

00:11:56.880 --> 00:12:03.720
Swimming nearer, the edges of my vision blur,
and I feel just a little dizzzzy.

00:12:03.720 --> 00:12:05.300
Huh.

00:12:05.300 --> 00:12:08.230
Has the sky always been that color?

00:12:08.230 --> 00:12:10.079
Everything feels…

00:12:10.079 --> 00:12:12.920
floaty for a while.

00:12:12.920 --> 00:12:18.899
Eventually, the effect wears off, and with
a clear head, I understand what just happened.

00:12:18.899 --> 00:12:23.750
That lifeform, known as the Lily Paddler,
possesses the unique ability to disorient

00:12:23.750 --> 00:12:28.149
would-be attackers with the mesmerizing patterns
on its colorful appendages.

00:12:28.149 --> 00:12:33.250
It's an impressive defense, and a vital one
for a creature like a Lily Paddler which is

00:12:33.250 --> 00:12:35.350
otherwise quite vulnerable.

00:12:35.350 --> 00:12:39.150
I just wish it didn’t make my head hurt.

00:12:39.150 --> 00:12:41.769
A shadow passes overhead.

00:12:41.769 --> 00:12:47.439
Just above me, a whale-sized leviathan opens
its mouth to a tremendous size and swallows

00:12:47.439 --> 00:12:51.709
an entire school of fish in a single gulp.

00:12:51.709 --> 00:12:57.120
This is a Glow Whale, and while they’re
highly effective at feeding on small fish,

00:12:57.120 --> 00:12:59.470
they’re thankfully quite gentle.

00:12:59.470 --> 00:13:05.279
At almost 100 feet, or 30 meters in length,
they’re the first Leviathan class organism

00:13:05.279 --> 00:13:09.630
I’ve encountered in Sector Zero — and
a highly intelligent one.

00:13:09.630 --> 00:13:16.139
A scan of the Glow Whales indicate they possess
many organs that are capable of bioluminescence,

00:13:16.139 --> 00:13:21.050
most notably around the corners of their eyes
and the ends of their horns.

00:13:21.050 --> 00:13:26.990
Careful not to startle it, I brush a Glow
Whale under its eye, causing a faint glow.

00:13:26.990 --> 00:13:31.710
It’s difficult to interpret whether this
is a random response, or an attempt to send

00:13:31.710 --> 00:13:32.949
a message.

00:13:32.949 --> 00:13:36.820
In any case, the Glow Whale is content with
my presence.

00:13:36.820 --> 00:13:42.850
As I watch it rejoin its pod, I wish I could
stay with this specular lifeform longer.

00:13:42.850 --> 00:13:49.010
But before the sun sets, there’s one last
region I need to chart…

00:13:49.010 --> 00:13:53.639
Underneath layers of snow and ice, there are
telltale signs that a permanent land mass

00:13:53.639 --> 00:13:56.110
makes up a portion of Sector Zero.

00:13:56.110 --> 00:14:01.290
Dry land is so scarce on this ocean planet
that it’s impossible to guess what sorts

00:14:01.290 --> 00:14:03.519
of lifeforms might live further inland.

00:14:03.519 --> 00:14:05.140
We’ll have to see for ourselves.

00:14:05.140 --> 00:14:10.960
Passing through a narrow canyon, I find strange
plant-animal hybrids that seem to be growing

00:14:10.960 --> 00:14:12.990
from the walls.

00:14:12.990 --> 00:14:19.130
These are Thermal Lilies, organisms that spend
most of the time tracking the Sun and absorbing

00:14:19.130 --> 00:14:23.459
its energy, but also turn to track my movement
as I pass.

00:14:23.459 --> 00:14:28.370
A scan suggests they aren’t dangerous, and
thanks to the thermal radiation they’ve

00:14:28.370 --> 00:14:33.420
absorbed, they’re actually a welcome source
of heat in Sector Zero’s frigid interior.

00:14:33.420 --> 00:14:38.699
Still, it’s unsettling the way they follow
me…

00:14:38.699 --> 00:14:43.910
Beyond the canyon, a snowy landscape far too
vast to traverse on foot awaits.

00:14:43.910 --> 00:14:47.870
Luckily, I have an alternate method of transportation.

00:14:47.870 --> 00:14:53.290
This is a Snowfox, a hoverbike designed for
land-based expeditions.

00:14:53.290 --> 00:14:58.089
Zooming over the arctic terrain, I find it’s
got a bit of a kick.

00:14:58.089 --> 00:15:02.499
At these speeds, I should be able to chart
this region fairly effectively.

00:15:02.499 --> 00:15:07.540
A short time later, I spot a hairy creature
in the distance.

00:15:07.540 --> 00:15:10.389
The question is, is it friendly?

00:15:10.389 --> 00:15:12.639
…Not friendly.

00:15:12.639 --> 00:15:17.959
Putting the Snowfox in reverse, I discover
the creature gives chase with alarming speed.

00:15:17.959 --> 00:15:22.680
After several near misses, I back away to
a safe distance.

00:15:22.680 --> 00:15:28.950
That quadrupedal predator was a Snow Stalker,
and while it nearly sank its claws into me,

00:15:28.950 --> 00:15:36.050
I can’t help but be excited at the discovery
of a terrestrial land animal on Planet 4546B!

00:15:36.050 --> 00:15:41.019
With its white fur and imposing bulk, the
Snow Stalker has converged on a body plan

00:15:41.019 --> 00:15:43.029
similar to Earth polar bears.

00:15:43.029 --> 00:15:46.410
I’m curious about the lineage of such a
lifeform.

00:15:46.410 --> 00:15:53.259
A brief analysis reveals the Snow Stalker
is actually a distant relative of the fully

00:15:53.259 --> 00:15:56.949
aquatic Stalker I encountered in the tropical
region.

00:15:56.949 --> 00:16:02.379
While these two species diverged a long time
ago, there are still some telltale signs of

00:16:02.379 --> 00:16:06.540
their relation in the shape of their head
and their dorsal spines.

00:16:06.540 --> 00:16:09.540
A fascinating genetic ancestry.

00:16:09.540 --> 00:16:14.639
Speaking of which, a short way off from the
Snow Stalker, I spot what appears to be its

00:16:14.639 --> 00:16:16.860
cub playing in the snow.

00:16:16.860 --> 00:16:22.220
Just a fraction of the size of its parent,
this baby Snow Stalker is far less aggressive

00:16:22.220 --> 00:16:23.910
than its parent.

00:16:23.910 --> 00:16:25.060
Well, mostly…

00:16:25.060 --> 00:16:31.959
Trekking onward, I reach an icesheet marked
by jagged, towering ice formations.

00:16:31.959 --> 00:16:37.100
This region is known as the Arctic Spires,
and it’s home to a giant…

00:16:37.100 --> 00:16:40.610
A huge head bursts from below the ice.

00:16:40.610 --> 00:16:46.500
Seconds later, a massive, serpentine leviathan
rises into the air, and nearly takes me out

00:16:46.500 --> 00:16:49.100
with a swipe from its massive horn.

00:16:49.100 --> 00:16:52.470
This terror of the frozen plains is an Ice
Worm.

00:16:52.470 --> 00:16:59.819
At over three hundred feet, or 90 meters,
it’s almost twice as long as a Reaper Leviathan,

00:16:59.819 --> 00:17:04.550
and by far the largest creature I’ve seen
in Sector Zero.

00:17:04.550 --> 00:17:09.589
Its glowing horn is superheated through an
exothermic chemical reaction — allowing

00:17:09.589 --> 00:17:13.530
it to melt the very ice its prey is standing
on.

00:17:13.530 --> 00:17:15.459
Time to make a fast exit.

00:17:15.459 --> 00:17:22.059
Speeding away on my Snowfox, I spot the Snowstalker
and its cub from earlier fleeing beside me.

00:17:22.059 --> 00:17:24.159
We barely make it to safety.

00:17:24.159 --> 00:17:28.309
With creatures like the Ice Worm around, I
think I might put off doing more field work

00:17:28.309 --> 00:17:31.240
till tomorrow.

00:17:31.240 --> 00:17:37.130
While the sun sets overhead, I check in to
find my Spy Pengling has become a veritable

00:17:37.130 --> 00:17:43.419
member of the flock, and has done an excellent
job keeping an eye on its feathered companions.

00:17:43.419 --> 00:17:49.330
Together, my pengling bot and I sit and watch
the Sector Zero sunset.

00:17:49.330 --> 00:17:53.010
And with the night comes a final surprise.

00:17:53.010 --> 00:17:59.600
The sky is lit up by a dazzling Aurora similar
to the Aurora Borealis in the northern latitudes

00:17:59.600 --> 00:18:00.600
of earth.

00:18:00.600 --> 00:18:05.559
On our planet, these ‘northern lights’
are a result of disturbances in the magnetosphere

00:18:05.559 --> 00:18:11.730
caused by solar wind — and we can theorize
they have the same origin on planet 4546B.

00:18:11.730 --> 00:18:17.850
It’s a beautiful, imposing display that
can make one feel a little isolated in the

00:18:17.850 --> 00:18:18.850
universe.

00:18:18.850 --> 00:18:22.201
But with my Spy Pengling beside me, I don’t
feel truly alone.

00:18:22.201 --> 00:18:26.590
A transmission interrupts my thoughts.

00:18:26.590 --> 00:18:30.809
It seems I’m picking up a mysterious communication…

00:18:30.809 --> 00:18:32.400
but from where?

00:18:32.400 --> 00:18:35.130
And who, or what, is sending it out?

00:18:35.130 --> 00:18:42.070
I’ll venture deeper into the depths in Part
Two of the Biology of Subnautica: Below Zero,

00:18:42.070 --> 00:18:46.960
investigating the source of the transmission,
and the larger, more extreme, and often more

00:18:46.960 --> 00:18:50.190
dangerous life of the deeper biomes.

00:18:50.190 --> 00:18:54.789
For now, though, our journey is at its end.

00:18:54.789 --> 00:19:01.070
As always, thanks for watching.

00:19:01.070 --> 00:19:05.710
If you enjoyed this entry, please lend your
support by liking, subscribing, and hitting

00:19:05.710 --> 00:19:10.120
the notification icon to stay up to date on
all things Curious.

00:19:10.120 --> 00:19:27.320
See you in the next video.

